 "captions": [
  {
   "t": "Your Gatorade got smaller. Same price. And you probably didn't notice.",
   "s": 0.05,
   "e": 4.2
  },
  {
   "t": "Gatorade swapped its thirty-two ounce bottle for twenty-eight ounces. That's twelve and a half percent less drink.",
   "s": 4.4,
   "e": 9.8
  },
  {
   "t": "Which means you pay about fourteen percent more per ounce. Without the price tag changing at all.",
   "s": 10.0,
   "e": 14.59
  },
  {
   "t": "It's called shrinkflation. Toblerone did it too. In twenty sixteen, the UK four-hundred-gram bar became three hundred sixty… by making bigger gaps between the triangles.",
   "s": 14.79,
   "e": 24.32
  },
  {
   "t": "Why do companies do it? Because we notice a higher price. But we almost never check the weight.",
   "s": 24.52,
   "e": 29.24
  },
  {
   "t": "The fix: look at the unit price on the shelf tag. Price per ounce never lies.",
   "s": 29.44,
   "e": 33.79
  },
  {
   "t": "What shrank on you? Tell me in the comments. Follow for more.",
   "s": 33.99,
   "e": 37.31
  }
 ],
